ed with that has HIV and  zika virus in it we can stack HIV and we  can also detect zika virus at a lower  titer using  within essentially fifteen minutes of  starting sequencing so nanopore  sequencing it does have several  advantages and I already mentioned most  of them it's portable it's pocket-sized  we can sequence RNA potentially in  addition DNA potentially they're very  fast turnaround times we could use these  in the field and in fact we've actually  deployed some of these nano port  sequences to Brazil and to Mexico to do  Zika virus sequencing the costs of the  sequencing tend to still be high and the  error rates are still high so these may  be potential disadvantages before we can  sort of see these technologies being  used materially in clinical setting I  want to end by acknowledging of the  multiple research collaborators and  scientists who work with me on this  they include members of my laboratory  including they're listed there support  from avid diagnostics we work at the  University of Calgary Davis lark coffee  and corn from rompe on the rhesus  macaque model Texas biomedical research  institute and Baylor School of Medicine  on marmoset model of Zika virus  infection the University of Oxford on  Zika virus genome sequencing and  analysis the calculate Department of  Public Health on investigation of  outbreaks Mexico the National Autonomous  University of Mexico on studying Zika  virus evolution as well as collaborators  in Barbados and Brazil and finally I  would like to acknowledge funding for my  research which includes funding from the  NIH the National Institutes of Health as  well as from Abbott Laboratories  thank you very much and I would be happy  to take any questions  Thank You dr. chu for that informative  presentation it's time for Q&amp;A if you  have a question you'd like to ask please  do so now just click on the green Q&amp;A  button at the lower left of the  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on your screen  and click on the send button will answer  as many of your questions as we have  time for if we are unable to get to your  question we will follow up with you via  email after the presentation let's get  started  our first question is what are the most  significant questions remaining  regarding a Zika virus so that's a good  question but what are what are kind of  the challenges that are remaining  involving Zika virus well there's so  much about the virus we don't understand  you know we don't really understand why  is it that some patients pregnant women  who are infected with Zika virus  actually have babies who have  microcephaly although this association  has now been proven and confirmed we  don't know what what is the risk of  microcephaly in a pregnant woman who's  infected with Zika virus we still don't  understand the risk of similarly the  risk of gamma ray syndrome in a patient  who is infected with Zika virus we don't  really understand kind of the mechanism  of sexual transmission of Zika virus why  is it that this is among all flavivirus  appears to be the only virus that  appears to be sexually transmitted and I  think what's really lacking with Zika  virus is that we really lack an  effective vaccine which is what we  really need to prevent these infections  ultimately as well as potential  treatments or or therapeutics that might  be used to either prevent or to mitigate  some of these consequences of Zika virus  infection and really what is really  needed it's kind of a broad-based  approach which involves both cell  culture understanding kind of the basic  biology of the virus of animal models  that could be used such as mouse models  and  give me primer muggles such as Aramis  that motto I I previously talked about  that could be used to to develop and  test drugs and vaccines candidate drugs  and vaccines as well as better ways to  diagnose the disease and to follow  patients who are infected with the virus  thank you the next question is why  aren't we seeing ongoing cases of Zika  in previous countries affected by the  outbreak this is a very interesting  question it is well it is now well  described differences that we have seen  very very few cases in Brazil we're  seeing very very few cases in areas that  have previously been essentially  severely affected by the Zika virus and  outbreak like Puerto Rico for instance  where much of the population may have  been exposed to the virus so whereas in  2014-2015 there were thousands of cases  in Brazil right now we're only seeing a  handful of cases in these locations now  we don't really know the actual answer  to this although it has been speculated  and I think there is some data to  support this that there may be of what  we call heard to be the effect meaning  that patients in these exposed areas or  individuals in these exposed population  have already been exposed to the virus  and therefore have developed an immunity  so are less likely to see cases of  transmission if they already immune I do  have to stress however that is unknown  whether or not kind of repeat infection  from Zika virus will help will prevent  is possible or whether or not it will it  will prevent any of the cont of the  downstream consequences of Zika virus  infection which is why I still feel that  efforts to develop a drugs or vaccines  to prevent infection in the first place  are still needed  thank you we wrap up with one more  question and that question is what kind  of diagnostics are needed for this  developing world in preparation for  outbreaks such as Zika virus that's a  great question in that's a great  question and what we really need for a  new Diagnostics for dealing with kind of  these new outbreaks we need broad-based  Diagnostics that can really detect all  types of organisms that cause these  diseases it's not just viruses that  cause disease although viral outbreaks  have probably received the most  publicity there have been cases of say  bacterial outbreaks there was actually  recently described in ongoing case cases  of a new fungal pathogen called Candida  aureus which has caused several  hospital-associated outbreaks throughout  the United States which is an ongoing  kind of outbreak situation that's been  monitored by the Centers for Disease  Control so what we really need are for  for doing this kind of surveillance or  being able to develop diagnostic tests  for for identifying outbreak patently is  you need tests that can identify the  full spectrum of infect infectious  pathogens that can cause these outbreaks  in other words we need a test that can  detect all viruses bacteria fungi and  parasites you know of which perhaps a  meta genomic test might be a suitable  candidate for doing so in addition you  need to have a test that could  potentially detect organisms or protect  detect sequences from organisms that are  potentially novel it's been well  described that many of these many of  these emerging outbreak pathogens  they're they either have have they're  either  they either arise from animals or they  haven't been  or their uncommon or they haven't been  described very well in the past and they  have been characterized very well and as  a result what you really want a  broad-based unbiased diagnostic tests  that could detect not only known  pathogens or well-characterized  pathogens but also kind of these novel  pathogens a good example of a novel  pathogen would be something like SARS  coronavirus which was a credible strain  that had previously not been appreciated  as causing infections in humans but  which is responsible for a global  outbreak you know several years ago so  what we would need is we think you need  probably broad-based diagnostic tests  that can detect the full spectrum of  pathogens as well as tests that could be  applied in the point-of-care sort of  directly in pandemic hotspots so that  diagnosis can be made quickly and  interventions public health and  infection control interventions could be  instituted immediately and in that way  hopefully be able to either prevent or  mitigate the spread of a potential  outbreak  thank you once again dr. chu I would  also like to thank lab roots for making  today's educational webcast possible  before we go I want to let everyone know  that today's webcast will be available  for on-demand viewing through December  14th 2017 you will receive an email from  lab roots letting me know when this  webcast will be available to replay  please share that announcement with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event that's all for now thank you  for joining us we hope to see you  against them goodbye
 

